linux-apps for Debian
=====================

Build configuration
-------------------

The upstream CMake build is OEM-specific: one binary set is produced per
(project, OEM) pair.  The packaging defaults to rw350r/dell.  To build another
target, pass the pair to dpkg-buildpackage, which forwards it to debian/rules:

  dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b "--eval=PROJECT:=rw350r" "--eval=OEM:=lenovo"
  dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b "--eval=PROJECT:=rw101"  "--eval=OEM:=lenovo"

or set DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS, which debian/rules unpacks itself:

  DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS="project=rw101 oem=lenovo" dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Both settings take the same values as the sections in rw101_config and
rw350r_config.  An unknown pair makes CMake abort the configuration.
debian/build-deb.sh wraps the common cases:

  ./debian/build-deb.sh rw101 lenovo

Source format: 3.0 (native)
---------------------------

debian/source/format is "3.0 (native)" and the version in debian/changelog is
the bare upstream version -- 2.1.9, with no -1 Debian revision.  The tree is
self-contained: there is no separate upstream release tarball that the
packaging patches, so dpkg-source builds ../linux-apps_2.1.9.tar.xz straight
from the working tree.

Consequences:

 * dpkg-buildpackage works with and without -b.  Under "3.0 (quilt)" with a
   "-1" version, dpkg-source demanded ../linux-apps_2.1.9.orig.tar.* and
   aborted unless -b was passed:

     dpkg-source: error: can't build with source format '3.0 (quilt)':
       no upstream tarball found - are you sure you want the native format?

 * debian/watch is deliberately absent.  uscan would hunt for an upstream
   tarball that the native format never consumes; upstream is reachable
   through the Homepage field in debian/control instead.

 * With no orig tarball, every file in the tree is shipped verbatim, so build
   artefacts must be excluded explicitly.  debian/source/options holds the
   tar-ignore list (build/, .git/, *.deb, *.dsc, *.changes, *.buildinfo,
   *.substvars, *.debhelper*).  Add new artefact paths there as well as in
   .gitignore -- dpkg-source does not read .gitignore.

 * CMakeLists.txt (project(... VERSION ...)) and debian/changelog have to
   carry the same version.  debian/verify-debian-pkg.sh checks that they agree
   and that the changelog version has no Debian revision.

Two build paths, one payload
----------------------------

There are two ways to produce the binary package:

  A) CPack (the upstream path)
       cmake -S . -B build -DBUILD_DEB=yes -DPROJECT_BUILD=rw350r \
             -DOEM_BUILD=dell -DBUILD_BY_LIB=1
       cmake --build build
       (cd build && cpack)

  B) debhelper (this directory)
       dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Both run the same CMake install rules over the same staged tree, so the
installed payload is identical: same paths, same modes, same contents.
debian/compare-with-cpack.sh builds both and diffs them, so any drift is caught
immediately.

Keeping it that way constrains the packaging in five ways, all of them
deliberate:

 * debian/rules disables dh_strip and dh_dwz.  CPack does not strip, so if
   debhelper stripped the ELF files the two payloads would diverge.  Enable
   stripping on both sides together if release binaries are needed.

 * debian/rules disables dh_installsystemd.  The maintainer scripts enable and
   start the units themselves (they have to, because CPack ships them without
   any debhelper snippets), and letting dh_installsystemd inject
   deb-systemd-helper calls would make the two postinst/postrm differ.

 * debian/{preinst,postinst,prerm,postrm} are byte-for-byte copies of
   script/{preinst,postinst,prerm,postrm}.  script/ is what CPack embeds via
   CPACK_DEBIAN_PACKAGE_CONTROL_EXTRA.  Edit script/ and re-copy; do not edit
   debian/ in place.

 * The "install" helper normalises the staged tree to 0755/0644 before both
   paths read it, and CMakeLists.txt deliberately sets no FILE_PERMISSIONS
   (an explicit mode there would override the staged one and affect only one
   of the two paths).

 * The install list is generated, not static.  dh_install aborts on a pattern
   that matches nothing, and the staged tree is target-dependent: the helper
   service directory is empty unless BUILD_DOWN_TOOL=yes, the MA helper only
   exists for BUILD_MA=yes, and /opt/rolling/rolling_fw_pkg is an empty drop
   directory.  debian/linux-apps.install.in is the complete list for all
   targets; debian/rules expands it into debian/linux-apps.install during
   override_dh_auto_install, dropping entries that matched nothing and
   creating the corresponding empty directories in the package tree (which is
   what CPack ships).  Anything staged that no entry claims is still caught by
   dh_missing, so a typo cannot silently drop a file.

The remaining difference is the documentation directory, which Policy 12.5
requires and CPack does not create:

  /usr/share/doc/linux-apps/copyright
  /usr/share/doc/linux-apps/changelog.Debian.gz
  /usr/share/doc/linux-apps/README.Debian.gz
  /usr/share/doc/linux-apps/README.md, NEWS

Layout: /opt/rolling
--------------------

Upstream installs the services under /opt/rolling.  FHS 3.0 permits a
distribution to install software in /opt ("Distributions may install software
in /opt, but must not modify or delete software installed by the local system
administrator"), and Debian Policy 9.1.1 only requires FHS conformance, so
this is not a Policy violation.  lintian reports dir-or-file-in-opt as a
warning; that warning is expected and accepted here.

Two subdirectories under /etc/opt/rolling are a genuine FHS deviation, and are
kept on purpose:

  /etc/opt/rolling/run            runtime state (FwFlashSrv)
  /etc/opt/rolling/rolling_fw_pkg firmware package drop directory

Both paths are compiled into rolling_flash (see
application/rolling_flash_service/rolling_flash_common.h), so moving them to
/var/opt/rolling requires a coordinated change to the C sources and to the
firmware packages that drop files there.  Doing it in the packaging alone
would produce a package whose service cannot find its own state.

ModemManager and fwupd integration
----------------------------------

Neither integration modifies files owned by another package:

 * The FCC unlock helpers are shipped under
   /opt/rolling/rolling_ma_service/fcc-unlock.d and copied into
   /usr/lib/*/ModemManager/fcc-unlock.d by postinst, using file names derived
   from the modem's idVendor:idProduct.  Only those names are ever removed
   again, never the directory and never a file belonging to modemmanager.

 * The --test-quick-suspend-resume / --test-low-power-suspend-resume flags are
   passed to ModemManager through a systemd drop-in
   (/etc/systemd/system/ModemManager.service.d/50-rolling-linux-apps.conf)
   instead of rewriting ModemManager.service with sed.  The packaged unit is
   left untouched, and the drop-in is removed on purge.

 * The fwupd fastboot quirks are written to
   /usr/share/fwupd/quirks.d/rolling-linux-apps.quirk instead of
   decompressing, appending to and recompressing fwupd's own
   builtin.quirk.gz.  fwupd reads every *.quirk file in that directory, so the
   quirks survive a fwupd upgrade.

Licensing
---------

The top-level LICENSE file is the single document that describes both the
source and the binary licensing; it is split the way the open-source review
asked for:

 * SECTION A -- the published source code (*.c, *.h, CMakeLists.txt,
   debian/, script/) is LGPL-2.1.  Exceptions: iniparser and safestringlib
   are MIT, qdl is BSD-3-clause, and
   application/include/common/rolling_list.h is GPL-2 (derived from the
   Linux kernel's include/linux/list.h).

 * SECTION B -- the Custom Binary Redistribution License covers the prebuilt
   artefacts: the four .a static archives in binary_deb/common_lib/ and the
   rolling_ma executable in application/rolling_ma_service/.  This
   repository publishes only the *_main.c entry points, the headers and the
   build integration; the ~24 translation units compiled into those
   artefacts (rolling_config_parse.c, rolling_flash_update.c,
   rolling_helper_adapter.c, dmidecode.c, ...) are not published, so the
   artefacts cannot be rebuilt from this tree.  The copyright holder grants
   redistribution in binary form through SECTION B instead; modification and
   source redistribution stay reserved.

   Each directory ships its own self-contained copy of that grant, listing
   file sizes and SHA-256 digests so that a copy can be checked against the
   original: binary_deb/common_lib/LICENSE for the archives,
   application/rolling_ma_service/LICENSE for rolling_ma.

   rolling_ma is not built here: application/rolling_ma_service/ holds the
   prebuilt binary and has no CMakeLists.txt, so application/CMakeLists.txt
   skips it and the upstream "install" helper copies the binary straight into
   the staging tree.

 * rolling_list.h is compiled into rolling_helper and into the two service
   archives, so those binaries are GPL-2 as a whole, as LGPL-2.1 section 3
   allows.

 * rolling_ma incorporates dmidecode-derived code (GPL-2).  SECTION B
   commits the copyright holder to providing that source on written
   request, as GPL-2 section 3 requires.  Legal should confirm the scope
   of that dmidecode derivation and the exact grantor entity
   ("Rolling Wireless S.a.r.l." in the current text).

Consequence for Debian: the prebuilt artefacts do not have their source in
the package, so they do not meet DFSG 2 -- the package is redistributable
(that is what SECTION B grants) but is not free software, and
debian/copyright declares binary_deb/* under the non-common license
"Rolling-binary-1.0".  If the package were ever uploaded to the Debian
archive it would have to go to non-free, or the artefacts would have to be
dropped and rebuilt from published sources.

Missing sources (Policy 4.16)
-----------------------------

Debian Policy 4.16 covers source packages that ship compiled objects whose
sources are not included: the sources must be shipped, either by repacking
the source tarball or by placing them in debian/missing-sources/.  This
package can do neither, and the gap is documented instead of papered over:

 * the ~24 translation units compiled into the four .a archives in
   binary_deb/common_lib/ and into rolling_ma are proprietary and are not
   published in this tree (see "Licensing" above), so the required sources
   do not exist inside the package;

 * debian/missing-sources/ therefore cannot be populated -- it would either
   be empty or hold placeholders, and both would misrepresent the situation
   to a reviewer worse than this section does.

Policy 2.2.4 requires non-free packages to "meet all policy requirements
presented in this manual that it is possible for them to meet"; the
compensating controls here are:

 * SECTION B of LICENSE grants binary-only redistribution explicitly and
   commits the copyright holder to providing the dmidecode-derived (GPL-2)
   source on written request, as GPL-2 section 3 requires;

 * binary_deb/common_lib/LICENSE and
   application/rolling_ma_service/LICENSE list file sizes and SHA-256
   digests, so any shipped artefact can be verified against the original;

 * debian/copyright declares the artefacts under the non-common license
   "Rolling-binary-1.0", and the "Consequence for Debian" paragraph under
   "Licensing" above records the archive-area decision this implies.

If the closed sources are ever published, they go under
debian/missing-sources/<component>/ and this section shrinks to a pointer;
until then it is the canonical answer to a reviewer asking where the
sources are.

Architecture
------------

The binary package is Architecture: amd64.  binary_deb/ ships only x86-64
prebuilt archives and an x86-64 rolling_ma, and debian/rules always configures
with -DBUILD_BY_LIB=1, so the package genuinely cannot be built for another
architecture.  Restore "Architecture: any" in debian/control once
per-architecture libraries are available for every supported architecture.
